WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.017 --> 00:00:02.120
- @Tough Cookie: "Stop
calling the symptoms

00:00:02.120 --> 00:00:04.607
of more than half the
population "atypical."

00:00:06.431 --> 00:00:09.720
(jazzy bass beat music)

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:12.220
Last week, not only did
I give you two videos

00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:14.690
that were quite deep in
terms of their topics,

00:00:14.690 --> 00:00:16.040
but they were quite long as well.

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:17.550
So I appreciate you watching them,

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:18.750
and leaving your feedback.

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:20.780
In today's video, I'd like to address

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:23.270
some of the most common
critiques and criticisms

00:00:23.270 --> 00:00:24.260
from those videos,

00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:27.160
in addition to responding
to comments as I usually do.

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:28.114
Let's get started.

00:00:28.114 --> 00:00:28.947
Peewoop!

00:00:28.947 --> 00:00:30.230
"STOP calling the symptoms

00:00:30.230 --> 00:00:32.700
of more than half the
population 'atypical.'"

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:35.400
I got this comment a lot,
and I totally understand why

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:38.350
The tricky part about
recognizing heart attack symptoms

00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:40.860
in female patients is
that they are often masked

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:42.650
and they often present atypically.

00:00:42.650 --> 00:00:44.600
It sounds like I'm calling the symptoms

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:46.380
of a heart attack atypical

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:48.620
because they happen in
the female population.

00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:49.470
That's not the case.

00:00:49.470 --> 00:00:51.900
The majority of all people, men and women,

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:53.890
experience heart attacks with chest pain.

00:00:53.890 --> 00:00:56.380
Now, if you're having
jaw pain, stomach pain,

00:00:56.380 --> 00:00:58.840
nausea, fatigue and you're
having a heart attack,

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:00.630
that is an atypical presentation,

00:01:00.630 --> 00:01:01.800
whether you're a male or a female.

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:03.460
What I was trying to say in the video

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:04.670
is that if you're a woman,

00:01:04.670 --> 00:01:07.070
you're more likely to have
these atypical symptoms,

00:01:07.070 --> 00:01:09.820
not that all women
experience atypical symptoms

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:11.660
and men experience classical symptoms.

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:12.997
That's not how that works.

00:01:12.997 --> 00:01:16.260
@Bella24NJ: "You mentioned
women use a more narrative

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:19.180
style of talking and that
makes doctors have more errors.

00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:20.770
Isn't that a form of victim blaming?"

00:01:20.770 --> 00:01:23.310
Bella, I can see how you can get there.

00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:25.270
What I think the study
was trying to accomplish

00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:27.700
was to figure out reasons why women

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:30.310
were being misdiagnosed
when it comes to the way

00:01:30.310 --> 00:01:31.640
they present their illnesses.

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:34.260
Now, this is not saying
that it's the woman's fault

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:36.090
for having this narrative style.

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:38.710
This study is identifying
that this is the cause

00:01:38.710 --> 00:01:40.250
and then we can further address it

00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:42.510
by educating doctors to be better suited

00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:45.080
to accept a type of presentation.

00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:47.340
Some advice that I give
to all my patients,

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:49.010
irrespective of their sex,

00:01:49.010 --> 00:01:51.870
is that they should not
be afraid to be outspoken.

00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:53.410
If a doctor's doing something

00:01:53.410 --> 00:01:54.660
that they're not comfortable with

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:56.950
or they feel that the
doctor is missing something,

00:01:56.950 --> 00:01:58.100
they should speak up.

00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:00.670
If that doctor is not willing
to listen and communicate,

00:02:00.670 --> 00:02:01.940
get a second opinion.

00:02:01.940 --> 00:02:04.200
That's not always feasible
in a given moment.

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:06.920
But it's important to
advocate for your own health.

00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:09.830
This is not to excuse bad doctors' habits.

00:02:09.830 --> 00:02:12.410
This is simply a practical solution

00:02:12.410 --> 00:02:14.737
for a problem that I see
occurring quite often.

00:02:14.737 --> 00:02:17.500
@JonsLana: "I hear you but
to be perfectly honest,

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:20.280
you sound like you are
justifying and making excuses.

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:21.970
I understand you are a medical provider,

00:02:21.970 --> 00:02:24.130
but please try and see this
from a patient's perspective.

00:02:24.130 --> 00:02:25.430
It would be more helpful."

00:02:26.410 --> 00:02:27.700
@JonsLana, I'm sorry you feel that way.

00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:30.180
I really do and try and
see all of these situations

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:31.660
from a patient's perspective.

00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:33.260
Not only have I been a patient,

00:02:33.260 --> 00:02:35.200
but my family have been patients.

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:36.660
I lost my mom to cancer,

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:38.460
and I spend a lot of time in hospitals.

00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:41.640
So I do know how the
system works intimately

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:44.380
from a professional perspective
and a patient's perspective.

00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:46.130
That being said, the purpose

00:02:46.130 --> 00:02:48.300
of me doing the reaction video to this

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:50.190
was not to defend doctors.

00:02:50.190 --> 00:02:53.660
It was simply to take
a 360 look at the issue

00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:56.710
and really point out that
while bias and discrimination

00:02:56.710 --> 00:02:58.890
definitely occur, we
need to talk about it,

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:01.050
I also didn't want to lose focus

00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:02.970
and see that there's a lot of other issues

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:05.060
that are contributing to these disparities

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:06.667
that weren't discussed on this bit.

00:03:06.667 --> 00:03:09.920
@JDela10: "I liked Haidt's
book, but I can't help

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:11.510
but see a bit of contradiction here

00:03:11.510 --> 00:03:13.800
because he says kids are antifragile

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:16.180
so don't overprotect them,
but yet he wants them

00:03:16.180 --> 00:03:18.290
to be kept away from
social media altogether."

00:03:18.290 --> 00:03:19.820
Jonathan Haidt made a really good point

00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:22.260
that we had to edit out
simply for time's sake

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:24.190
because this was 100-plus-minute interview

00:03:24.190 --> 00:03:25.950
and we did only show you 48 minutes.

00:03:25.950 --> 00:03:27.510
So I'll let him say his take.

00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:29.440
But I have a slightly different
opinion on the matter.

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:31.691
- Okay, Haidt, you're saying that we need

00:03:31.691 --> 00:03:33.260
to let kids out more
and not supervise them

00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:34.570
and kids have to climb trees

00:03:34.570 --> 00:03:35.403
and they have to get injured.

00:03:35.403 --> 00:03:36.236
But then you're saying

00:03:36.236 --> 00:03:37.700
we shouldn't let them on social media.

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:39.460
Aren't you coddling them
and overprotecting them

00:03:39.460 --> 00:03:40.810
from social media?

00:03:40.810 --> 00:03:43.430
And I say, well yeah,
logically that's a good point.

00:03:43.430 --> 00:03:45.550
But let's see if it's the
same kind of learning.

00:03:45.550 --> 00:03:47.050
When you climb a tree
and you get in trouble,

00:03:47.050 --> 00:03:49.080
you get scared or you
fall or hurt yourself,

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:50.650
you learn, you don't avoid trees.

00:03:50.650 --> 00:03:53.254
You actually climb trees
better the next time.

00:03:53.254 --> 00:03:55.470
But when you're shamed on
social media, what happens?

00:03:55.470 --> 00:03:58.063
And I always ask, say,
everybody who's in Gen Z,

00:03:59.250 --> 00:04:00.150
how many of you have been on it

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:01.380
since you were 10 or 11 years old?

00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:02.540
A lot of hands go up.

00:04:02.540 --> 00:04:05.960
I say, okay, do you think that
being on social media early

00:04:05.960 --> 00:04:08.370
and being shamed and being attacked,

00:04:08.370 --> 00:04:10.070
do you think that overall
that's made you tougher

00:04:10.070 --> 00:04:11.910
and more independent
because I've been shamed

00:04:11.910 --> 00:04:13.920
and attacked so many times, I don't care,

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:17.070
or do you think it's
made you more hesitant,

00:04:17.070 --> 00:04:18.550
it's made you more self-censoring,

00:04:18.550 --> 00:04:20.680
it's made you more careful
about what you say?

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:23.050
And the overwhelming consensus,

00:04:23.050 --> 00:04:25.210
it's a large majority say it's the former,

00:04:25.210 --> 00:04:27.303
that they, no, no, the latter, I'm sorry.

00:04:27.303 --> 00:04:28.136
- Yes.

00:04:28.136 --> 00:04:28.969
- The large majority say

00:04:28.969 --> 00:04:29.802
- Yes.

00:04:29.802 --> 00:04:30.635
- That it's that they're more

00:04:30.635 --> 00:04:31.468
- Protective.

00:04:31.468 --> 00:04:34.100
- They're more self-censoring,
basically more cowardly.

00:04:34.100 --> 00:04:37.160
- In my opinion, we don't
want to expose children

00:04:37.160 --> 00:04:40.200
to unnecessary chronic
stress or chronic trauma.

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:42.410
And I think social media
falls into that category.

00:04:42.410 --> 00:04:44.710
If my child goes to school
and gets called a name

00:04:44.710 --> 00:04:46.080
and comes home upset about it,

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:47.830
that's good, that's an acute stress.

00:04:47.830 --> 00:04:49.240
They're gonna learn how to deal with that.

00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:50.870
But now if they're being called that name

00:04:50.870 --> 00:04:53.420
every single day at school, being bullied,

00:04:53.420 --> 00:04:55.010
then they come home and
they're being bullied

00:04:55.010 --> 00:04:56.790
on social media and it never stops,

00:04:56.790 --> 00:05:00.450
that now crosses a line
into chronic stress/trauma

00:05:00.450 --> 00:05:01.960
where I need to step in.

00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:03.250
My second point on social media

00:05:03.250 --> 00:05:05.170
is that it really functions like a drug

00:05:05.170 --> 00:05:07.340
because of the way that
it affects our minds.

00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:09.330
The likes create a dopamine hit,

00:05:09.330 --> 00:05:13.890
much like vape pens do, cigarette
smoking, alcohol use does.

00:05:13.890 --> 00:05:16.620
And we don't let young children use those

00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:19.470
because they haven't quite
developed the frontal cortex,

00:05:19.470 --> 00:05:21.540
the decision-making part of their brain.

00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:24.060
So exposing them to
social media earlier on

00:05:24.060 --> 00:05:25.940
can really affect the way they mature

00:05:25.940 --> 00:05:27.260
and their circuits form.

00:05:27.260 --> 00:05:29.940
So I think keeping children
away from social media

00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:31.060
until they're mature,

00:05:31.060 --> 00:05:32.760
and that line is different for everybody,

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:33.867
is not a bad idea.

00:05:33.867 --> 00:05:36.520
"This is the first time I've
turned off one of your videos.

00:05:36.520 --> 00:05:38.700
The implication that children
shouldn't be protected

00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:41.470
from bullying, or emotional
abuse, is ridiculous.

00:05:41.470 --> 00:05:43.110
It doesn't make children stronger.

00:05:43.110 --> 00:05:45.570
But it does make many of
them suicidal, including me."

00:05:45.570 --> 00:05:47.950
Now Sarah, I'm incredibly sad to hear that

00:05:47.950 --> 00:05:50.320
and I'm sorry you had that
experience watching our video.

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:53.290
The type of conversation that
Jonathan Haidt are having

00:05:53.290 --> 00:05:56.630
is really fraught with nuance
because it's really important

00:05:56.630 --> 00:05:58.500
that we don't just say, "Oh, all children

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:00.680
should be bullied and
they'll become stronger

00:06:00.680 --> 00:06:01.780
and that's all good."

00:06:01.780 --> 00:06:03.090
That's not what we're saying.

00:06:03.090 --> 00:06:03.923
And it's really important

00:06:03.923 --> 00:06:05.730
that we pay attention
to that bit of nuance.

00:06:05.730 --> 00:06:08.110
What we're saying is that acute stress,

00:06:08.110 --> 00:06:09.920
small amounts of struggle,

00:06:09.920 --> 00:06:12.040
challenges are good for children.

00:06:12.040 --> 00:06:13.500
What we don't want to see happen

00:06:13.500 --> 00:06:16.740
is children exposed to
serious traumatic events,

00:06:16.740 --> 00:06:18.550
chronic abuse or bullying

00:06:18.550 --> 00:06:21.400
because in that case it changes
from a healthy challenge

00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:22.750
to an unhealthy challenge.

00:06:22.750 --> 00:06:25.210
Now, that line is
different for every child,

00:06:25.210 --> 00:06:27.580
based on how they've developed maturity,

00:06:27.580 --> 00:06:30.480
based on what experiences
they've had in their lives,

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:32.140
based on their parents even.

00:06:32.140 --> 00:06:33.590
And that's really up to the parent

00:06:33.590 --> 00:06:35.160
to decide where that line lies.

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:37.710
Both too much and too little intervention

00:06:37.710 --> 00:06:39.447
can have adverse effects.

00:06:39.447 --> 00:06:41.500
@fero: "mike: I am a real doctor.

00:06:41.500 --> 00:06:43.210
Also mike: I don't know my blood type."

00:06:43.210 --> 00:06:46.060
Oh, my God, there were so
many comments about this.

00:06:46.060 --> 00:06:48.800
It is perfectly normal to
not know your blood type.

00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:51.730
For example, if I have a
patient come into the office

00:06:51.730 --> 00:06:53.560
and I order them a complete blood count,

00:06:53.560 --> 00:06:56.050
a complete metabolic panel, vitamin test,

00:06:56.050 --> 00:06:59.420
cholesterol panel, thyroid
panel, whatever it is,

00:06:59.420 --> 00:07:01.340
it doesn't automatically come in

00:07:01.340 --> 00:07:02.660
to tell me their blood type.

00:07:02.660 --> 00:07:04.720
That's a special test
that I have to order.

00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:06.997
And guess what, your
insurance is gonna say,

00:07:06.997 --> 00:07:08.560
"Well, why did you need to know that?

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:09.710
Why are we paying for it?

00:07:09.710 --> 00:07:11.470
If it's not of medical necessity,

00:07:11.470 --> 00:07:12.510
you have to pay for it."

00:07:12.510 --> 00:07:14.370
The one time that your
insurance will pay for it

00:07:14.370 --> 00:07:15.930
that will apply to a lot of people

00:07:15.930 --> 00:07:17.630
is in a preoperative setting.

00:07:17.630 --> 00:07:20.336
So if you come to me and
you're about to go for surgery

00:07:20.336 --> 00:07:21.420
and you need clearance,

00:07:21.420 --> 00:07:23.520
I'm the one that's gonna
be doing that clearance,

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:26.800
I may order a blood test just to make sure

00:07:26.800 --> 00:07:29.060
that if something happens
during the operation,

00:07:29.060 --> 00:07:32.877
we have your blood type ready
to go and save your life.

00:07:32.877 --> 00:07:35.520
@Panda Red: "My health
economy teacher told me

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:39.437
to always be positive
but never HIV-positive."

00:07:40.810 --> 00:07:42.600
If you're HIV-positive, that's okay too.

00:07:42.600 --> 00:07:43.690
We can figure it out.

00:07:43.690 --> 00:07:46.370
In fact, the control for
HIV has gotten so good

00:07:46.370 --> 00:07:48.180
that the life expectancy for someone

00:07:48.180 --> 00:07:51.890
who takes treatment carefully
and properly and appropriately

00:07:51.890 --> 00:07:54.900
for HIV is essentially
the same life expectancy

00:07:54.900 --> 00:07:57.567
as for the average person
of that same sex and age.

00:07:57.567 --> 00:08:00.330
@Kim Carpenter: "I have
epilepsy and I was wondering

00:08:00.330 --> 00:08:03.486
what goes on in my brain
whilst that happens."

00:08:03.486 --> 00:08:04.810
Kim, that's a really great question.

00:08:04.810 --> 00:08:06.028
Now, I'm not a neurologist.

00:08:06.028 --> 00:08:07.072
But I'll sort of give you

00:08:07.072 --> 00:08:08.651
a very basic explanation of what happens.

00:08:08.651 --> 00:08:09.484
In one part of your brain,

00:08:09.484 --> 00:08:12.480
there happens to be an
area of over-excitation,

00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:13.930
almost like an electric storm.

00:08:13.930 --> 00:08:17.090
And then, that starts spreading
throughout your entire brain

00:08:17.090 --> 00:08:18.810
and that's how you can develop epilepsy

00:08:18.810 --> 00:08:20.330
and go into a full-on seizure

00:08:20.330 --> 00:08:22.210
where you're shaking,
you're feeling things,

00:08:22.210 --> 00:08:23.730
seeing things or confused

00:08:23.730 --> 00:08:26.250
because it activates all
different parts of your brain.

00:08:26.250 --> 00:08:29.440
Interestingly enough, if
patients fail medical therapy,

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:30.650
meaning taking all these types

00:08:30.650 --> 00:08:32.540
of medications for their seizures,

00:08:32.540 --> 00:08:33.820
one of the possible treatments

00:08:33.820 --> 00:08:35.260
is surgical intervention,

00:08:35.260 --> 00:08:37.300
literally taking out the part of the brain

00:08:37.300 --> 00:08:39.130
where we've isolated the beginning

00:08:39.130 --> 00:08:42.117
of that seizure activity to
that pinpoint part of the brain.

00:08:42.117 --> 00:08:44.940
@Regan RN: "Dr. Mike,
where is the difference

00:08:44.940 --> 00:08:48.610
between an MD and a DO
and which is better?"

00:08:48.610 --> 00:08:51.300
You've phrased your
question in the exact title

00:08:51.300 --> 00:08:53.440
that I have a video already existing.

00:08:53.440 --> 00:08:55.220
And that video just hit a million views.

00:08:55.220 --> 00:08:56.890
I'm gonna link it down
below in the description.

00:08:56.890 --> 00:08:59.650
It's called, "What is the
difference between an MD and a DO

00:08:59.650 --> 00:09:00.483
and which is better?"

00:09:00.483 --> 00:09:02.350
I'm very passionate about this subject.

00:09:02.350 --> 00:09:03.183
I've actually partnered

00:09:03.183 --> 00:09:05.290
with the American Osteopathic Association,

00:09:05.290 --> 00:09:09.860
which represents well over
100,000 practicing DOs

00:09:09.860 --> 00:09:12.080
and we're gonna be giving
all sorts of little tidbits

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:14.530
about the differences and
what makes a DO special.

00:09:14.530 --> 00:09:15.517
So stay tuned for that.

00:09:15.517 --> 00:09:18.160
@Carolyn Byce: "Video
idea: Can you do a video

00:09:18.160 --> 00:09:20.250
about surgery anxiety/what to expect?

00:09:20.250 --> 00:09:21.580
I'm having surgery for the first time

00:09:21.580 --> 00:09:22.670
and I am really nervous."

00:09:22.670 --> 00:09:24.610
Caroline, this is such a good idea.

00:09:24.610 --> 00:09:26.550
Part of my job as a family medicine doctor

00:09:26.550 --> 00:09:28.410
is to do pre-surgical clearances,

00:09:28.410 --> 00:09:29.930
which means that right before a patient

00:09:29.930 --> 00:09:31.810
gets to go under the knife,

00:09:31.810 --> 00:09:33.310
they come to me first to make sure

00:09:33.310 --> 00:09:34.930
that they're healthy
enough to go for surgery

00:09:34.930 --> 00:09:36.650
or at least optimized well enough

00:09:36.650 --> 00:09:37.500
to go for surgery.

00:09:37.500 --> 00:09:40.300
During this process, I have
really in-depth conversations

00:09:40.300 --> 00:09:41.190
about their fears,

00:09:41.190 --> 00:09:43.480
about what they need
to be doing beforehand.

00:09:43.480 --> 00:09:46.490
And I think that can make for
a really good quality video.

00:09:46.490 --> 00:09:48.590
I know what questions most people have.

00:09:48.590 --> 00:09:50.270
And I know what language I need to use

00:09:50.270 --> 00:09:51.420
to explain that to them.

00:09:51.420 --> 00:09:53.140
If you want that type of video,

00:09:53.140 --> 00:09:54.690
someone's gotta be the leader here

00:09:54.690 --> 00:09:57.010
and put a really good
comment down below this video

00:09:57.010 --> 00:09:58.360
and up-vote that bad boy.

00:09:58.360 --> 00:10:01.130
Let's make the limit
for it 5,000 up-votes.

00:10:01.130 --> 00:10:04.317
If you get that, dedicated
video to presurgical anxiety.

00:10:04.317 --> 00:10:07.220
@Addy Paul: "Question: What
happens if you stay upside down

00:10:07.220 --> 00:10:08.740
for an extremely long time?"

00:10:08.740 --> 00:10:10.310
You're gonna get a headache

00:10:10.310 --> 00:10:12.500
because gravity is gonna
change the amount of blood

00:10:12.500 --> 00:10:13.370
that's going to your head.

00:10:13.370 --> 00:10:15.100
And you're gonna get a lot
more blood pooling there.

00:10:15.100 --> 00:10:16.650
Now, your legs are used to this

00:10:16.650 --> 00:10:18.930
because obviously you're
standing and upright

00:10:18.930 --> 00:10:20.370
for the majority of the day.

00:10:20.370 --> 00:10:21.870
In fact, the veins in your legs

00:10:21.870 --> 00:10:25.530
have natural valves that as
each time your heart pumps,

00:10:25.530 --> 00:10:27.420
those valves open and then close

00:10:27.420 --> 00:10:30.270
to make sure that the blood
doesn't automatically fall

00:10:30.270 --> 00:10:31.750
and pool to the bottom of your legs.

00:10:31.750 --> 00:10:34.080
You may see this in some
people as they get older,

00:10:34.080 --> 00:10:36.160
when those valves become incompetent

00:10:36.160 --> 00:10:38.380
or they have damage to their veins

00:10:38.380 --> 00:10:41.040
and you see that their legs
are constantly swollen all day.

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:43.330
That's because blood is pooling down there

00:10:43.330 --> 00:10:45.387
and their venous system
is a little bit abnormal.

00:10:45.387 --> 00:10:48.730
@Luckzle: "Health question why can't I see

00:10:48.730 --> 00:10:50.557
when I close my eyes?"

00:10:52.260 --> 00:10:53.480
Because you have eyelids

00:10:54.410 --> 00:10:55.940
and they're not transparent.

00:10:55.940 --> 00:10:57.260
There's actually some animals I know

00:10:57.260 --> 00:10:58.740
that have transparent eyelids

00:10:58.740 --> 00:11:01.850
and then a regular eyelid
so they can see underwater.

00:11:01.850 --> 00:11:03.080
If you know what kind of animal that is,

00:11:03.080 --> 00:11:04.599
drop it down below in the comments

00:11:04.599 --> 00:11:05.530
because I need a refresher.

00:11:05.530 --> 00:11:07.583
I think it's a toad or a snake.

00:11:09.340 --> 00:11:10.893
Oh, alligators.

00:11:12.310 --> 00:11:13.143
Whatever.

00:11:13.143 --> 00:11:16.000
@Jennifer Saunders: "This man
really just pulled out nuts

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:19.080
out of his dishwasher, put
them in a drinking glass."

00:11:19.080 --> 00:11:20.610
There are way too many jokes

00:11:20.610 --> 00:11:22.710
to be made about that entire situation.

00:11:22.710 --> 00:11:25.430
Man, like I was so tired and delirious

00:11:25.430 --> 00:11:26.570
when I was filming that.

00:11:26.570 --> 00:11:28.850
Yes, I store my nuts in my dishwasher

00:11:28.850 --> 00:11:30.970
and I put them in a drinking glass.

00:11:30.970 --> 00:11:31.803
Deal with it.

00:11:31.803 --> 00:11:34.310
@Jocelyn Raj: "Dr. Mike: 'I love nuts.'

00:11:34.310 --> 00:11:36.510
Fans: start mailing
all kinda nuts to him."

00:11:36.510 --> 00:11:39.190
Actually, if you want to send
me some interesting nuts,

00:11:39.190 --> 00:11:40.410
I'm down.

00:11:40.410 --> 00:11:42.010
I've gotta be careful when
I say things like that.

00:11:42.010 --> 00:11:43.550
I feel like I'm gonna go to my P.O. Box

00:11:43.550 --> 00:11:45.400
and be unpleasantly surprised.

00:11:45.400 --> 00:11:46.800
- What's in the box!

00:11:46.800 --> 00:11:48.480
- I know many of you were scared off

00:11:48.480 --> 00:11:51.410
by the 48-minute mark on
my latest anxiety video.

00:11:51.410 --> 00:11:53.603
But I urge you, make the time.

00:11:54.482 --> 00:11:55.589
Check out this video.

00:11:55.589 --> 00:11:56.960
Leave me your comments
because I'm still very active

00:11:56.960 --> 00:11:58.290
in that comments section.

00:11:58.290 --> 00:12:00.880
And as always, stay happy and healthy.

